In calm air, a typical paraglider moves about 8 to 10 m forward for every 1 m it drops. Launch from a hill 1,000 m high and, with no rising air at all, you could glide roughly 8 to 10 km before landing. That is the distance of a long autorickshaw ride across a city.

Pilots steer by pulling on brake lines that bend the back edge of the wing. Pull the left brake and you turn left.

{{story:gliders}}

India's fishing boats are as varied as its coastline. On the Coromandel Coast of Tamil Nadu and Andhra Pradesh, fishers used the kattumaram, logs tied together into a raft that rides over the surf. Its name [[@words-borrowed|gave English the word]] "catamaran". Kerala has long, narrow wooden vallams. In [[@kochi|Kochi]], giant cantilevered fishing nets, said to have come with traders from China centuries ago, still dip into the water along the shore.

On the coast of Gujarat, sailors from Mandvi and nearby ports built wooden dhows and sailed them to Arabia and East Africa. Their navigators, called malams, steered by the stars, the winds and the colour of the sea. In [[@mumbai|Mumbai]], the Koli fishing community lived on the islands long before the city grew around them.

{{story:monsoon}}

Jellyfish have soft bodies, so they hardly ever turn into [[fossil|fossils]]. But in rocks in Utah, USA, scientists have found prints of jellyfish about 505 million years old. Some even show faint traces of [[tentacle|tentacles]].

That makes jellyfish older than [[@sharks-old|sharks]], older than trees and much older than the dinosaurs. Their close cousins, the corals, have been building reefs for hundreds of millions of years too, as you can read in [[@coral-reefs|coral reefs]].

{{story:ageing}}

Today [[@ships-tech|the largest container ships]] are about 400 m long, longer than three football fields, with room for more than 20,000 of the smaller containers. Even so, they [[@floating-ships|float]], because their huge hulls push aside an enormous weight of water.

At the dock, ship-to-shore cranes taller than a 15-storey building lift each box. A skilled operator can move a container every minute or two. Computers plan exactly where every box goes, keeping heavy ones low and balancing the ship so it doesn't tip. Each container has its own code, and its journey is tracked from the factory gate to the shop.

{{story:floating-ships}}

This simple system finds short routes. Ants that happen to take a shorter path get back faster, so their trail gets more scent sooner. More ants choose it, and it becomes stronger still. Longer paths slowly lose out.

Computer scientists noticed. In the early 1990s, a researcher named Marco Dorigo wrote "ant colony" [[@algorithms|algorithms]], computer programs in which imaginary ants leave digital scent. These programs are used to plan delivery routes and network traffic.

{{story:algorithms}}

Around 1010 CE, the Chola king Rajaraja I completed the [[@india-wonder-brihadeeswara|Brihadisvara Temple]] in Thanjavur. Its walls carry carvings of dance poses described in the ancient *Natya Shastra*. At the Nataraja temple in Chidambaram, the gateway towers show all 108 of these poses, called *karanas*.

These carvings show how close dance and temple life once were. Dancers today still visit these temples to study the poses.

{{story:cholas}}

Raag Bhairav, named after a fierce form of Shiva, is one of the best-known morning ragas. Its mood is grave and peaceful, a calm way to open the day. Raag Yaman, an evening raga, is one of the first that many students learn, because its notes are clear and its mood is gentle.

Late at night comes Darbari Kanada, slow and grand. Its name comes from *darbar*, a royal court, and tradition connects it with Tansen, the great singer at Emperor [[@akbar|Akbar]]'s court. The deep, swaying notes are meant for the quiet hours when the city has gone to sleep.

{{story:day-night}}

In March 1990, dozens of disabled activists in Washington, D.C., left their wheelchairs and crutches at the bottom of the US Capitol steps. They pulled themselves up the 83 marble steps with their arms, to demand a new law. People called it the "Capitol Crawl".

Months later, the Americans with Disabilities Act became law. It requires buildings, buses and workplaces to be accessible. In 2006, the [[@united-nations|United Nations]] agreed a [[treaty|treaty]] on disability rights, and India passed its own law in 2016.

{{story:braille}}

To reach the best summer grass, flocks must cross high passes. In the Dhauladhar range above Dharamshala, routes like the Indrahar Pass rise above 4,000 m. Snow can linger there into summer. A late storm can trap a flock for days.

Gaddi herders traditionally wear a thick woollen coat called a chola, tied with a long rope of black wool called a dora. They sleep in the open or in small tents and rock shelters, with dogs curled around the flock.

{{story:art-pashmina}}

Worker bees, usually a couple of weeks old, have [[gland|glands]] on the underside of the abdomen. These squeeze out tiny clear flakes of wax. The bees chew the flakes to soften them, then press them into walls about as thin as a sheet of paper.

Each cell is also tilted slightly upward, towards the back of the comb. That small slope helps stop runny [[@honey|honey]] from dripping out before the bees cap the cell with more wax. The same comb serves as nursery, larder and dance floor, where bees pass on directions to flowers in [[@bees-dance|their waggle dance]].

{{story:bees-dance}}

In 1903 the champions of two rival leagues agreed to play each other. The Boston Americans, later the Red Sox, beat the Pittsburgh Pirates. That series was best of nine.

In 1904 the New York Giants refused to play, saying the American League was not good enough, so there was no series. In 1994 a strike by players cancelled the end of the season, and again there was no World Series. Those are the only two missing years.

{{story:probability}}

One of India's early cricket heroes was Palwankar Baloo, a left-arm spinner who played in the 1890s and 1900s. He was a Dalit, from a community treated as "untouchable", and was often forced to eat and drink apart from his teammates. His skill made him famous anyway. In 1937 he stood for election in Bombay against [[@ambedkar|B.R. Ambedkar]], the great leader who fought against caste discrimination.

At the same time, a prince named Ranjitsinhji was dazzling crowds in England with his stylish batting. India's national championship, the Ranji Trophy, started in 1934, is named after him.

{{story:trade-routes}}

Pure gold is called 24-carat. It is beautiful but soft enough to scratch with a fingernail. Eighteen-carat gold is 18 parts gold to 6 parts other metals, such as copper and silver, mixed into an [[alloy|alloy]]. That makes it much harder and better at surviving a dressing room full of excited players.

Some scientists have calculated that if the trophy were solid gold, it would weigh far more than 6.2 kg. So it is believed to be hollow, though FIFA hasn't said. The [[@gold-where|gold]] in it may have come from mines anywhere in the world.

{{story:gold-where}}

In the early 1970s, an engineer named Frank Nasworthy began selling skateboard wheels made of polyurethane, a tough, slightly soft [[@plastic-made|plastic]]. Suddenly boards gripped the road and rolled smoothly over cracks. Skateboarding exploded in popularity.

At the same time, a long [[drought|drought]] in California left many backyard swimming pools empty. Skaters in Santa Monica and Venice, near [[@los-angeles|Los Angeles]], began riding up and down the curved pool walls. Their bold style created vert skating, where riders fly above the edge of a ramp.

{{story:lever-lift}}

A little stress can help. In 1908, two American psychologists, Robert Yerkes and John Dodson, found that mice learned a task best with a moderate level of pressure, not too little and not too much. The idea has been stretched since then, but many people do notice they focus better with a little nervousness.

Athletes, musicians and actors feel butterflies before big moments too. Many say the trick isn't to get rid of the feeling, but to see it as their body getting ready. Some actors in the [[@theatre|theatre]] say that if they stopped feeling nervous before a show, they'd worry something was wrong.

{{story:sundarbans-tigers}}

An emu is Australia's tallest bird and the world's second tallest after the ostrich. It stands up to about 1.9 m, taller than most grown men. It can't fly, but its long legs carry it at about 50 km/h, faster than Usain Bolt at full speed.

Emus are also tough. Their thick, shaggy feathers and strong bodies meant that a hit didn't always stop one. Like all birds, emus are descended from dinosaurs, a connection explained in [[@birds-dinosaurs|birds and dinosaurs]].

{{story:birds-dinosaurs}}

Most families used wagons pulled by oxen, which were slower than horses but tougher and cheaper. With canvas stretched over wooden hoops, the wagons looked like ships on a sea of grass, so people called them prairie schooners.

The wagons were so full that hardly anyone rode in them, and they had no springs, so riding was bumpy anyway. People walked, often 20 km a day. Children herded cattle, collected dried buffalo dung for fuel and kept an eye on smaller siblings. Some walked barefoot when their shoes wore out.

{{story:lewis-clark}}

The International Kolkata Book Fair began in 1976 and has grown into one of the biggest public book fairs in the world. Held over about two weeks in late January and early February, it draws visitors in the millions. People in Kolkata talk about it the way some cities talk about a cricket match.

Each year a different country is the theme, and its pavilion is often designed to look like a famous building. Bengali publishers launch new titles, little magazines sell poetry for a few rupees, and students argue about authors over cups of tea. When a fire destroyed many stalls in 1997, the fair reopened within days.

{{story:libraries}}

In 1857, members of the Philological Society in London called for a new dictionary that would trace the full history of every English word. Volunteers across the English-speaking world read old books and posted in slips of paper with quotations showing how words had been used. Millions of slips arrived.

The editor, James Murray, worked in an iron shed in his garden in Oxford that he called the Scriptorium. One of the busiest volunteers was an American former army [[surgeon|surgeon]] named William Chester Minor. He sent thousands of slips from a hospital in England for people with mental illness, where he lived for decades. The first full edition of the *Oxford English Dictionary* was finished in 1928, about 70 years after the project began.

{{story:printing-press}}
